<p>This bothered me a bit. I think it would flow better if it were either &quot;whose appearance is similar to that of Spanish moss etc.&quot; or &quot;similar in appearance to Spanish moss etc.&quot;</p>
<p>Also, I was slightly confused as to what exactly &quot;SCP-1826&quot; referred to. The containment procedures seem to imply that it is the actual building, whereas the description itself uses it to designate either the phenomenon as a whole or the entity possessing those who enter the building during the month of March.</p>
<p>Edit: Upon rereading, this sentence also jumped up at me:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No anomalous properties were present in the gun, and is of modern manufacture with the serial number 196001430, which trace back to a resident of Belarus believed to have gone missing in the area in 2005.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Is&quot; has no grammatical subject (it's fairly obvious you're referring to the gun, but the construction is rather jarring), and &quot;trace&quot; should probably be &quot;traces&quot; since &quot;the serial number&quot; is singular.</p>
<p>Final edit, I swear: Can one really be armed with a gas mask? Wouldn't something like &quot;equipped&quot; be more appropriate?</p>

							</div>

								
